From dbd13ad72ca08fda56364b07badf8c176a37054c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ws07 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 23:11:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/18] Web client (S1): an arrival's comparison names its
 basis; the intake and the comparison judged apart
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CARAVAN_SENT's cargo is after the towers' wear, so "all arrived" against it
read as if what was first loaded had come through whole. A delivery is now
"all (or part) of the send record's amount", with the basis said: what was
first loaded and what the road took are not in the record, and nothing is
worked out for them (the record's wear rate is quoted as written). Amounts
are in the records' units (cargo_log drops what is under one).

Whether the intake is known and whether it can be compared with the send
are two judgements:
- intake: a delivered outcome, to a village, under the S1 program's rules
  (PROGRAM_VERSION_S1 = 4: Holding::credit adds the record's cargo whole).
  A storehouse, an unread destination, another program version or an
  unread outcome leave it not verified.
- comparison: the send read, to the very same place (kind, province, site,
  generation, tile). With no send read, or with a send to another place
  (a taken caravan, a site's next village), the intake is told alone
  (verifiedIntake).

The lead of a settlement's notice no longer says 決着しました twice (隊商が決着しました…決着しました) —
it names the caravan and the turn, and the state's words say the rest.

Regression tests keep a burned caravan (logged delivered, empty) and a
taken one from ever telling their original sender an arrival. Applies on
0001-0010.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
